  "query_preview": "../Tuesday through Friday/...Active weather looks to continue\nfor the long term period with multiple lows moving into the Gulf \nand subsequently past the panhandle bringing more rain and wind to\nthe area. Starting Tuesday evening, the larger low which is \nexpected to be near Kodiak will have a smaller, more compact low \npotentially in the around 970mb moving northward before phasing \nwith the original circulation Tuesday morning. This will bring a \nreinforcing shot of moisture to the panhandle as well as more \nwinds to the area. With cold air at 850mb in Canada sitting around\n-7C, it is possible that the northern panhandle could see some \nmixed precipitation, especially places not at sea level. \n\nBehind these two lows, a third circulation looks for move into the \narea Tuesday night into early Wednesday morning which will bring \nanother reinforcing round of moisture and wind. Once these lows \ndissipate, our attention then turns towards the potential of a low \nmoving up the Aleutian chain and into the western Gulf Thursday. \nCurrent ensemble guidance shows this system crossing the Gulf Friday \ninto Saturday while higher pressure remains the Interior and the \nYukon. One thing that will need to be watched is if an of high \npressure does develop as some ensemble members are hinting at. But \nat this moment, more members are agreement that a high does not \nform. The big area of uncertainty is the exact track and strength of \nthis low as there is some divergence regarding this feature. Getting \ntowards the end of the week, with some of the cooler air spilling \nover from Canada, it will be more possible to see some mixed \nprecipitation for places like Haines, Skagway, and potentially \nYakutat starting Thursday. The Icy Strait corridor could see some \nmixed potentially for next weekend if the guidance continues with \nthe colder overnight lows.",
